    text: "The 1920s in America, often called the Jazz Age, was a period of remarkable cultural transformation. Jazz music, with its improvised rhythms and African-American roots, swept through the country and beyond, becoming the soundtrack of a generation rebelling against the conservative values of the previous era. Speakeasies filled with dancers, flappers challenged gender norms, and the spirit of optimism seemed boundless. This was also the decade of Prohibition, which paradoxically fuelled a thriving underground economy centred on illegal alcohol. Writers like F. Scott Fitzgerald captured the glittering excess and underlying emptiness of this era with extraordinary precision.",
